	abstract = {This paper presents ZenIA, a low-cost, AI-enabled assistive robot designed to support elderly individuals in home settings with medication adherence, basic companionship, and responsive interaction. ZenIA integrates on-device face recognition, 360-degree LiDAR-based navigation, and an automated medication dispensing mechanism, coupled with a lightweight software stack that prioritizes privacy through local data storage and offline operation. The mechanical design emphasizes safety and ergonomics (rounded geometries) for domestic environments, while the human–robot interaction (HRI) layer combines synthesized speech and a 7-inch touch GUI to improve usability for older adults and caregivers. We detail the hardware–software architecture, including Raspberry Pi 5, Arduino, HuskyLens AI camera, LD-19 LiDAR, and a servo-based carousel dispenser. Technical validation shows 87% face-recognition accuracy in a small cohort, reliable obstacle avoidance in mixed indoor layouts, and synchronized medication dispensing. A preliminary user session with a 70-year-old participant validated task completion and acceptability. We discuss cost/robustness/privacy trade-offs, limitations (lighting sensitivity, lack of global SLAM, minimal user sample), and future steps toward multimodal fall detection, human-aware navigation, and broader living-lab trials in Latin America. ©2025 IEEE.},
